codex
1. Performance hypothesis: `event-fast-par` runs `parallel_benchmark` through headless workers on small partitions, so fixed per-update bookkeeping matters more than raw node execution. The worker was already creating `EventFastEngine` with `render:false` and `benchmark:true`, but the runtime still paid timeline-hook overhead and detailed debug-counter increments on the hot path.

2. What changed: I only edited [engine-fast-runtime.js](C:/Users/suzuk/AppData/Local/Temp/fact-sim-auto-patch/1773543404925-25116/js/app/engine-fast-runtime.js#L150) and [engine-fast-runtime.js](C:/Users/suzuk/AppData/Local/Temp/fact-sim-auto-patch/1773543404925-25116/js/app/engine-fast-runtime.js#L288). `EventFastEngine` now caches `renderEnabled` and skips timeline capture/draw when `render:false`, and it suppresses detailed hot-path counter updates when `benchmark:true`. Protected `dt`/`event`, engine-test code, harnesses, and unrelated files were left untouched.

3. Focused verification: `node --check js/app/engine-fast-runtime.js` passed. On `parallel_benchmark` with `metrics benchmark wallMs=500`, the pre-change run was `7532.320x`; post-change runs were `7793.632x`, `8372.256x`, and `6846.976x` for a mean of `7670.955x` (`+1.8%` versus that pre-run, but noisy). `engine_test` `quick` and `standard` both passed for `dt` vs `event-fast-par` with `strictFinalParity=true`, `reruns=2`, `stopOnFirstFailure=true`. I did not run `cd mcp && npm run check` because this patch workspace only contains the engine files.

4. Residual risk: the 500ms benchmark is materially noisy in this environment, so the speed win is directional rather than perfectly stable, and the measured mean is still below the request’s `9726.176x` target here. Also, benchmark-mode debug counters such as heap/node/queue totals are now intentionally less populated; this should not affect parity, but any tooling that inspects those counters during benchmark runs should be watched.
